                    <note xml:lang="" resp="nurbiczek" type="">
                        <p>Abū al-Ṭayyib Šalom is a Qaraite figure, attested in RNL Yevr.-Arab. I 1151. This calendar chronicle documents the Qaraite practice of determining the calendar empirically. Abū al-Ṭayyib Šalom is mentioned as conducting field inspections of barley crops in Palestine, both alone as well as together with another Qaraite authority, <emph rend="italic">al-muʿallim</emph> Abū Saʿīd (possibly Abū Saʿīd Levi b. Yefet). The aim was to determine whether the barley had reached the stage of <emph rend="italic">aviv</emph>, a prerequisite for declaring the first month of the year and celebrating Passover, or whether the year should be intercalated by adding an extra month. Contrary to other Qaraites, Abū al-Ṭayyib Šalom applied a stricter criterion and decided to intercalate in 415 AH / 1024 CE, aligning with a stricter faction (<emph rend="italic">kabbāsūn</emph>). This illustrates the coexistence of divergent Qaraite calendrical practices and how members (<emph rend="italic">aṣḥābunā</emph>) of the Qaraite academy in Jerusalem were involved in the process of intercalation during the first half of the eleventh century.</p>
                    </note>
